```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on router1, the core transit router, in the FRR routing daemon
subsystem — specifically the RIP daemon (ripd) and its configuration files.

The `/etc/frr/frr.conf` file is absent on router1, and `ripd.conf` is
zero-bytes (empty with no `router rip` block or network statements). Because
the FRR systemd service requires these files to bootstrap the daemon suite,
the service entered a `failed` state at startup. As a result, only the zebra
kernel-interface process (pid 62) is running; ripd never launched.

Without ripd, router1 participates in no routing protocol. Its kernel FIB
contains only two directly-connected entries (10.16.1.0/24 on eth2 toward
router0, 10.20.1.0/24 on eth3 toward router2) and two static kernel routes.
It has zero knowledge of any remote subnet (172.16.x, 172.20.x, 192.168.x,
etc.). Any transit packet arriving from the router0 side destined for the
router2/router3 side (or vice versa) is dropped by router1 for lack of a
forwarding entry, producing the "partial loss of reachability between zones"
symptom while intra-zone traffic to each router's local gateway remains
functional.

The ticket's claim that "the cable between router0 and router1 dropped" is
incorrect. Both `router0.eth2` (10.16.1.1/24) and `router1.eth2`
(10.16.1.2/24) are link-state UP with confirmed bidirectional traffic
(non-zero RX/TX byte counters on both sides).

Root cause: The FRR configuration files on router1 were deleted/zeroed
(frr.conf missing, ripd.conf empty), causing the FRR service to fail and
leaving router1 with no active routing protocol.

Corrective fix: Restore `/etc/frr/frr.conf` and `/etc/frr/ripd.conf` on
router1 with the appropriate `router rip` block and network statements
covering its connected subnets (at minimum `network 10.0.0.0/8`), then
run `systemctl restart frr` to bring ripd back up and restore RIP
convergence across the transit path.
=== END ===
